Here's the recommended approach using `Window` lifecycle events and `Preferences`:

**1. Create a custom `Window` subclass:**

```csharp
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page) { }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NoteViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", vm.DraftText);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NoteViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                vm.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NoteViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", vm.DraftText);
        }
    }
}
```

**2. Return it from `App.CreateWindow`:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
}
```

**Key things to know:**

- **`OnStopped`** fires when the app is fully backgrounded — this is where you save.
- **`OnResumed`** fires when returning from background — this is where you restore.
- **`OnDestroying`** also saves because on Android the back button can skip `Stopped` entirely.
- **`Resumed` does NOT fire on first launch** — the initial sequence is `Created → Activated`. Load your initial data normally in your page/viewmodel constructor.
- **Keep handlers fast** (< 1–2 seconds) — Android enforces a ~5s ANR timeout, and iOS has a background watchdog. `Preferences` is ideal for small values like draft text.
